1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,410
At the end of November 2022, a web
page appeared with a small box on it.

2
00:00:06,220 --> 00:00:09,510
You typed a question.
And a computer answered.

3
00:00:10,356 --> 00:00:14,516
Not with a pre-written reply. With
a sentence. It could write an email.

4
00:00:14,596 --> 00:00:18,546
Explain mathematics.
Translate a page. Write code.

5
00:00:19,352 --> 00:00:22,822
And it could hold something that felt
uncomfortably like a conversation.

6
00:00:23,732 --> 00:00:25,572
It was called ChatGPT.

7
00:00:26,272 --> 00:00:30,562
And for millions of people, this was the
moment artificial intelligence began.

8
00:00:33,352 --> 00:00:36,442
Except a computer had done
something like this before.

9
00:00:37,624 --> 00:00:39,054
Fifty-six years earlier.

10
00:00:39,901 --> 00:00:43,821
In 1966, a researcher at MIT called Joseph

11
00:00:43,901 --> 00:00:46,831
Weizenbaum published a
program called ELIZA.

12
00:00:47,528 --> 00:00:50,718
You could type: "My boyfriend says
I'm depressed much of the time."

13
00:00:51,388 --> 00:00:54,718
And ELIZA would answer: "I am
sorry to hear you are depressed."

14
00:00:55,568 --> 00:00:58,378
And you could carry on.
And so would the program.

15
00:00:59,342 --> 00:01:01,112
ELIZA was not intelligent.

16
00:01:01,762 --> 00:01:03,312
It was not a neural network.

17
00:01:04,082 --> 00:01:05,672
It understood nothing at all.

18
00:01:06,375 --> 00:01:10,425
It matched keywords in what you typed
and handed the rest back as a question.

19
00:01:11,375 --> 00:01:14,485
The whole script fitted in the
appendix of a journal paper.

20
00:01:15,327 --> 00:01:17,077
People talked to it anyway.

21
00:01:18,804 --> 00:01:21,054
Weizenbaum's own secretary
had watched him build it.

22
00:01:21,644 --> 00:01:23,534
She knew exactly what it was.

23
00:01:24,353 --> 00:01:27,623
After a few exchanges with it,
she asked him to leave the room.

24
00:01:28,193 --> 00:01:30,083
She wanted to be alone with it.

25
00:01:32,743 --> 00:01:36,753
Which leaves a question that the
next half century kept walking into.

26
00:01:37,553 --> 00:01:40,603
How little does a machine
actually need to seem intelligent?

27
00:01:43,793 --> 00:01:46,483
Somebody had already
asked a version of that.

28
00:01:47,053 --> 00:01:48,443
Sixteen years earlier.

29
00:01:49,453 --> 00:01:54,343
In 1950, the British mathematician Alan
Turing opened a paper with a question.

30
00:01:55,179 --> 00:01:56,529
"Can machines think?"

31
00:01:57,349 --> 00:01:59,099
And then he did something clever.

32
00:01:59,909 --> 00:02:01,729
He decided the question was too vague to

33
00:02:01,809 --> 00:02:04,199
answer, and swapped it
for a different one.

34
00:02:04,871 --> 00:02:08,121
Put a person and a machine at
the far end of a conversation.

35
00:02:09,231 --> 00:02:11,491
If you cannot tell which
is which, does it still

36
00:02:11,571 --> 00:02:13,941
matter whether the machine
is really thinking?

37
00:02:14,740 --> 00:02:16,590
It is an elegant argument.

38
00:02:16,800 --> 00:02:20,090
And ELIZA is what happens
when you take it literally.

39
00:02:20,939 --> 00:02:26,169
Because ELIZA convinced people in 1966,
and there was nothing behind it at all.

40
00:02:28,859 --> 00:02:31,279
Six years after Turing's paper, a group of

41
00:02:31,359 --> 00:02:33,949
scientists spent the summer
at Dartmouth College.

42
00:02:34,639 --> 00:02:38,349
Their proposal used a phrase that
nobody had written down before.

43
00:02:39,159 --> 00:02:40,409
Artificial intelligence.

44
00:02:41,653 --> 00:02:43,523
And their ambition was enormous.

45
00:02:44,553 --> 00:02:46,453
If we could describe human
intelligence precisely

46
00:02:46,533 --> 00:02:50,103
enough, we could program
a machine to reproduce it.

47
00:02:50,732 --> 00:02:55,322
A second idea had been around even
longer, and it pointed the opposite way.

48
00:02:56,161 --> 00:02:59,781
In 1943, a neurophysiologist
and a logician

49
00:02:59,861 --> 00:03:03,071
wrote down a simple model
of a biological neuron.

50
00:03:03,916 --> 00:03:05,326
It took a few numbers in.

51
00:03:05,416 --> 00:03:08,216
Gave each one a weight. Added them up.

52
00:03:08,296 --> 00:03:10,486
And decided whether to fire.

53
00:03:11,376 --> 00:03:13,526
On its own it can do almost nothing.

54
00:03:14,373 --> 00:03:16,163
Its weights were fixed by hand.

55
00:03:16,693 --> 00:03:18,913
Two years after Dartmouth, a psychologist

56
00:03:18,993 --> 00:03:21,483
built one that could
work them out for itself.

57
00:03:22,386 --> 00:03:24,556
And the idea underneath that was this.

58
00:03:24,766 --> 00:03:27,876
Maybe we do not program
intelligence at all.

59
00:03:28,226 --> 00:03:32,776
Maybe we build enough simple units, wire
them together, and let the system learn.

60
00:03:35,306 --> 00:03:38,996
None of it worked. Not
because the ideas were wrong.

61
00:03:39,106 --> 00:03:41,876
Because the machines
were nowhere near ready.

62
00:03:42,638 --> 00:03:43,988
Computers were slow.

63
00:03:44,138 --> 00:03:45,478
Memory was tiny.

64
00:03:45,558 --> 00:03:47,988
And there was almost
nothing to learn from.

65
00:03:48,378 --> 00:03:52,128
Training a large network was not
difficult. It was impossible.

66
00:03:53,042 --> 00:03:57,592
And there was a second problem, which was
worse, because nobody had predicted it.

67
00:03:58,282 --> 00:04:02,412
A computer could do arithmetic faster
than every human who had ever lived.

68
00:04:03,188 --> 00:04:05,448
But ask it to do something
a five-year-old does

69
00:04:05,528 --> 00:04:08,338
without thinking about
it, and it fell apart.

70
00:04:10,023 --> 00:04:15,513
Recognise a dog in a photograph. For a
person, instant. For a computer, hopeless.

71
00:04:16,504 --> 00:04:19,924
Eventually the money
noticed. Funding was cut.

72
00:04:20,004 --> 00:04:21,414
The excitement faded.

73
00:04:22,224 --> 00:04:25,044
The field went through long
stretches that later earned a name.

74
00:04:25,124 --> 00:04:27,194
AI winters.

75
00:04:27,977 --> 00:04:30,467
Artificial intelligence did not disappear.

76
00:04:31,137 --> 00:04:32,707
It just stopped being interesting.

77
00:04:35,637 --> 00:04:38,307
What changed was not a brilliant new idea.

78
00:04:39,157 --> 00:04:44,147
From the 1990s onwards, computers got
dramatically faster and storage got cheap.

79
00:04:44,908 --> 00:04:50,138
And the world began producing enormous
amounts of digital data. Web pages.

80
00:04:50,228 --> 00:04:53,778
Photographs. Scanned books. Video. Text.

81
00:04:54,587 --> 00:04:57,677
For the first time there was
something for a machine to learn from.

82
00:04:57,927 --> 00:05:00,537
And the question quietly turned over.

83
00:05:01,255 --> 00:05:04,325
Not: "How do we program all of
the rules into the computer?"

84
00:05:04,895 --> 00:05:08,565
But: "What if we hand it the data
and let it work out the rules?"

85
00:05:11,115 --> 00:05:16,985
In 1997, a machine called Deep Blue beat
Garry Kasparov, the world chess champion.

86
00:05:17,803 --> 00:05:19,173
It was enormous news.

87
00:05:19,723 --> 00:05:22,573
And it is worth being
precise about what it proved.

88
00:05:23,263 --> 00:05:25,433
Deep Blue could not hold a conversation.

89
00:05:25,983 --> 00:05:28,173
It could not recognise a dog.

90
00:05:28,623 --> 00:05:29,973
It could not learn anything.

91
00:05:30,771 --> 00:05:33,721
It was extraordinarily
good at exactly one thing.

92
00:05:34,391 --> 00:05:36,581
ELIZA looked intelligent and was not.

93
00:05:37,371 --> 00:05:39,861
Deep Blue was superhuman
and still was not.

94
00:05:40,685 --> 00:05:42,755
Both of them broke the same assumption.

95
00:05:43,225 --> 00:05:46,735
That if something looks like thinking,
there must be thinking behind it.

96
00:05:49,175 --> 00:05:53,955
Now, the dog. In 2012, a neural
network called AlexNet was

97
00:05:54,035 --> 00:05:56,205
pointed at more than
a million photographs.

98
00:05:57,251 --> 00:05:58,861
It was not a new idea.

99
00:05:59,571 --> 00:06:02,981
Neural networks were older than
most of the people working on them.

100
00:06:03,664 --> 00:06:07,134
What was new was everything
around it. Enough data.

101
00:06:07,284 --> 00:06:08,714
Enough computing power.

102
00:06:09,084 --> 00:06:10,974
Better ways to train it.

103
00:06:11,679 --> 00:06:12,949
It did not just win.

104
00:06:13,719 --> 00:06:17,209
It won by a margin that made the
older approaches look finished.

105
00:06:18,299 --> 00:06:20,039
The thing a five-year-old could do and a

106
00:06:20,119 --> 00:06:23,269
computer could not, a
computer could suddenly do.

107
00:06:24,003 --> 00:06:25,673
And the question changed again.

108
00:06:26,363 --> 00:06:28,053
Not "can neural networks work?"

109
00:06:28,303 --> 00:06:30,233
But "how far do they go?"

110
00:06:32,443 --> 00:06:36,313
In 2017, a paper came out
of Google with an odd title.

111
00:06:37,243 --> 00:06:38,773
"Attention Is All You Need."

112
00:06:39,583 --> 00:06:43,413
It described a new architecture for
neural networks, called the transformer.

113
00:06:44,233 --> 00:06:46,823
It let a model read a
whole sentence at once,

114
00:06:46,993 --> 00:06:49,423
and work out which words
belonged with which.

115
00:06:50,260 --> 00:06:56,740
Almost everything you have heard of since
then is built on it. GPT. BERT. Claude.

116
00:06:56,820 --> 00:07:01,630
Gemini. And still, outside the
field, nobody had noticed anything.

117
00:07:02,351 --> 00:07:06,121
OpenAI began building a
family of models called GPT.

118
00:07:08,821 --> 00:07:12,011
Each one was larger than the last
and better at the same trick.

119
00:07:12,521 --> 00:07:15,171
Given some text, guess what comes next.

120
00:07:16,045 --> 00:07:20,315
By GPT-3 that trick had turned into
something nobody quite expected.

121
00:07:21,305 --> 00:07:27,835
It could answer a question. Finish an
argument. Write an essay. Generate code.

122
00:07:28,757 --> 00:07:30,707
And almost nobody used it.

123
00:07:31,477 --> 00:07:32,847
It was a research tool.

124
00:07:33,457 --> 00:07:36,407
You needed an account, some
documentation, and a reason.

125
00:07:37,177 --> 00:07:40,467
The technology was
there. The door was not.

126
00:07:42,737 --> 00:07:48,307
On the 30th of November 2022,
OpenAI put a text box on a web page.

127
00:07:49,105 --> 00:07:50,895
That is close to the whole story.

128
00:07:51,803 --> 00:07:54,373
It was not a new kind of
artificial intelligence.

129
00:07:55,383 --> 00:07:57,373
It was not the first neural network.

130
00:07:57,583 --> 00:08:00,433
And it certainly was
not the first chatbot.

131
00:08:01,162 --> 00:08:04,352
ELIZA was doing that job
fifty-six years earlier.

132
00:08:05,119 --> 00:08:10,169
What changed was that you no longer
had to understand any of it. You typed.

133
00:08:10,699 --> 00:08:11,899
It answered.

134
00:08:12,366 --> 00:08:15,276
Five days later, a million
people had signed up.

135
00:08:17,676 --> 00:08:21,336
When people say AI started
with ChatGPT, it is a

136
00:08:21,416 --> 00:08:25,826
little like saying the internet
started with Google. It did not.

137
00:08:26,416 --> 00:08:29,326
Google just made it
enormously easier to use.

138
00:08:30,149 --> 00:08:34,939
And underneath ChatGPT there is a list,
and every item on it took decades.

139
00:08:36,005 --> 00:08:40,145
Computing power. Huge amounts
of data. Neural networks.

140
00:08:40,225 --> 00:08:43,045
Better ways of training
them. Transformers.

141
00:08:43,125 --> 00:08:44,935
And finally, a simple interface.

142
00:08:47,775 --> 00:08:50,825
So when did artificial
intelligence actually begin?

143
00:08:51,669 --> 00:08:53,459
2022? No.

144
00:08:54,746 --> 00:08:56,716
2017? Not really.

145
00:08:57,951 --> 00:09:00,101
1956? Closer.

146
00:09:01,034 --> 00:09:04,764
But even in 1956 nobody
was starting from nothing.

147
00:09:05,634 --> 00:09:08,344
The models of neurons
were already written down.

148
00:09:08,914 --> 00:09:11,804
Turing had already asked
whether the question made sense.

149
00:09:12,697 --> 00:09:14,497
And people had been telling stories about

150
00:09:14,577 --> 00:09:18,147
artificial minds for a
very long time before that.

151
00:09:18,830 --> 00:09:20,840
There is probably no first day.

152
00:09:23,970 --> 00:09:28,410
Think about that secretary
at MIT in 1966, asking to

153
00:09:28,490 --> 00:09:31,360
be left alone with a few
pages of pattern matching.

154
00:09:32,173 --> 00:09:34,883
The machines have become
unimaginably better.

155
00:09:35,553 --> 00:09:37,703
We have not become much
harder to convince.

156
00:09:40,323 --> 00:09:44,093
So perhaps ChatGPT was not the
beginning of the age of AI.

157
00:09:44,883 --> 00:09:48,393
Perhaps it was simply the moment
everybody finally noticed.

158
00:09:49,338 --> 00:09:53,128
And perhaps the interesting question
was never when we invented this.

159
00:09:53,918 --> 00:09:56,728
It is how long it takes us to
work out what we have built.
